1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,548
Sam had Harlan's first avatar video open in Ads Manager, ready to schedule.

2
00:00:04,548 --> 00:00:05,612
It looked finished.

3
00:00:05,612 --> 00:00:07,011
It wasn't allowed to run.

4
00:00:07,011 --> 00:00:11,657
The avatar (an AI-generated on-screen presenter, standing in for an actor) had come

5
00:00:11,657 --> 00:00:16,359
from a stock library's cheapest plan, and that plan covered personal, organic posts,

6
00:00:16,359 --> 00:00:17,423
not paid campaigns.

7
00:00:17,423 --> 00:00:21,733
Nobody had checked the licence tier, what the plan you're paying for actually

8
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:25,260
permits you to do with the footage, before generating the clip.

9
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:29,514
Sam caught it before a dollar of spend, which is the only reason it became a

10
00:00:29,514 --> 00:00:32,089
five-minute fix instead of an account problem.

11
00:00:32,089 --> 00:00:35,224
This section is built around not repeating that mistake.

12
00:00:35,224 --> 00:00:39,814
It's about UGC video, short ads styled like an ordinary customer's own clip rather

13
00:00:39,814 --> 00:00:42,612
than a polished commercial, built without a shoot.

14
00:00:42,612 --> 00:00:46,979
Every lecture ahead assumes you check rights before you generate, never after.

15
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:49,585
Five lectures, and the order matters.

16
00:00:49,585 --> 00:00:54,086
Rights and the avatar landscape come first, because generating first and checking

17
00:00:54,086 --> 00:00:57,309
rights after is exactly how Harlan's first draft got made.

18
00:00:57,309 --> 00:01:01,643
Scripting comes next, then the actual generation, properly licensed this time.

19
00:01:01,643 --> 00:01:06,200
The section closes on assembly: turning a generated clip into something that works

20
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:09,701
with the sound off, which is how most people actually watch it.

21
00:01:09,701 --> 00:01:13,536
Before lecture one, do the five-minute version of the check yourself.

22
00:01:13,536 --> 00:01:18,148
Open whichever avatar or stock tool you already use and find its licence or pricing

23
00:01:18,148 --> 00:01:18,426
page.

24
00:01:18,426 --> 00:01:23,038
That's the exact five minutes that would have caught Harlan's first draft, and it's

25
00:01:23,038 --> 00:01:25,539
the cheapest insurance in this whole section.
